WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.902
This pattern finds the latest admission for each patient.

00:00:04.902 --> 00:00:09.652
For the current patient, the inner query finds the maximum admission date.

00:00:09.652 --> 00:00:16.207
The outer admission row is kept when its date equals that patient-specific maximum.

00:00:16.207 --> 00:00:20.707
This pattern is useful for latest order, latest event, newest status, or most recent activity.

00:00:20.707 --> 00:00:25.627
If two rows share the same maximum date, both can be returned.

00:00:25.627 --> 00:00:31.517
Connect this idea to a compliance workflow checking whether related evidence exists.

00:00:31.517 --> 00:00:37.162
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:37.162 --> 00:00:45.017
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:45.017 --> 00:00:50.812
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:50.812 --> 00:00:56.795
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
